Introduction to IP

- like a shipping truck that moves a large amount of data
- network topology is the road
- data that are carried is called packets
- the truck is the Internet Protocol or IP
- the boxes that the truck carries hold your data and the boxes can be categorized as TCP and UDP types
- inside of these boxes are application information

In the world of networking, this is called encapsulation and decapsulation

=====================================================================================================================================================================================================================================================

TCP and UDP - also called OSI Transport Layer 4
- transported inside of IP or encapsulated
- two ways to move data from place to place or called multiplexing

TCP means Transmission Control Protocol
- connection oriented
- a formal connection setup and close, example like a phone call
- called "reliable" delivery as well, can manage out of order messages or retransmissions if data got corrupted while the transportation process
- includes an acknowledgement process after the data has been received or data sending
- also can perform flow control where the receiver can manage how much data is sent
- like a two way interaction

UDP means User Datagram Protocol
- connectionless
- no formal open or close to the connection
- also called "unreliable" delivery, we never know if the receiver received the data because there is no built-in acknowledgedment on the logic, just receive function
- no reordering of data or retransmissions
- no flow control, sender determines the amount of data transmitted
- one way interaction only
- this technology can be used for real-time communication like VoIP and video conversation

Connectionless protocols related to UDP
- DHCP or Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol used for automatically assigning IP addresseses for all the devices on the network
- TFTP or Trivial File Transfer Protocol used for sending small amounts of data within the network
- the application that uses the UDP protocal may have the setting to keeps track or record what happened with the real-time communication
